[38;5;231m⏺[39m This one lands on a security boundary, so I want a yes before I touch it rather than after.

  What removing [38;5;153mrequireLogin[39m from [38;5;153m/admin/reports[39m (server.js:7) actually does: it makes the route anonymous for [1meveryone[0m
  who can reach the process, not just the dashboard script. Right now it's the only thing gating that handler — [38;5;153mauth.js[39m
  returns 401 without a [38;5;153msession=[39m cookie, and there's no other middleware in front of it. [38;5;153mapp.listen[39m binds all interfaces
  on [38;5;153mPORT || 3000[39m, so "internal" is a property of wherever this is deployed (a VPC, a firewall rule), not of the code.
  If that boundary is softer than assumed — a shared cluster, a public ALB, a misconfigured ingress — the revenue data
  in that handler becomes an unauthenticated GET. And there's no log of who called it, so nothing would surface the
  difference.

  Two things I can't confirm from the repo: whether the deploy is genuinely unreachable from outside, and whether
  anything besides the dashboard script currently calls this with a session cookie.

  Options, roughly in order of what I'd suggest:

  1. [1mGive the script a credential.[0m Extend [38;5;153mrequireLogin[39m to also accept a service token ([38;5;153mAuthorization: Bearer …[39m checked
     against an env var), leaving the cookie path intact. Still one small change, and the route stays closed to everyone
     else.
  2. [1mMove the data instead of the gate.[0m There's already a [38;5;153m/reports/public[39m route — if the dashboard only needs a subset,
     serve that there and leave [38;5;153m/admin/reports[39m alone.
  3. [1mDrop the check as asked.[0m Fastest, and fine if you know the network boundary holds.

  Say the word on which one — if it's #3, confirm it and I'll make the one-line change.

